  "story": {
    "bet": [
      {
        "text": "Have a simple robot working as early as possible, then iterate all season; the goals centered on a simple, reliable robot for the district level.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s5",
          "s33"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "demand": [
      {
        "text": "At kickoff the low hub was non-optional: low risk, competitive points, best for the cargo ranking point, harder to defend. In tight and elimination matches the team expected a reliable upper hub scorer to be stronger. Low and mid rungs were must-haves; traversal only a could-have.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s24"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "A week later passing under the low rung became a must.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s25"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "auto": [
      {
        "text": "Five cargo (February version): shoot two, then one, then two taken at the terminal, always ending at 14.9 s, without vision.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s28",
          "s29"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "A steal routine hits a staged cargo across the midline with a shot from the intake, then collects it; run on Einstein, it helped win a match by four points.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s30",
          "s47"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "cycle": [
      {
        "text": "A flexible over-bumper intake feeds omni wheels that center cargo into the tower; a color sensor lets it eject opponent cargo. The driver stops at an opening, and hood angle and flywheel speed follow the distance from odometry.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s26",
          "s30",
          "s31",
          "s32"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "endgame": [
      {
        "text": "The telescopes pull the robot up; passive hooks take its weight and tilt, and the telescopes hook the next rung on the swing. One button runs a timed sequence. After the redesign, missed traversals at Greater Boston came from late timing or partners in the way.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s36",
          "s38",
          "s31"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "fallback": [
      {
        "text": "Override switches on the operator console select shooter presets when odometry is inaccurate. Shorter autos, from four cargo down to one, were kept in case the complex ones were unreliable.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s34",
          "s28"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "gave_up": [
      {
        "text": "A west coast drive with no turret made accurate odometry harder to keep through a match when not shooting; the fender auto-drive was not used in competition because odometry was not reliable enough between target detections.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s34",
          "s40"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Three of the four failures it lists at the championship were on the intake, which went to the top of the off-season redesign list.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s33"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "changed": [
      {
        "text": "Before week 1: one top shooter roller became three, to cure bounce-out. For Greater Boston: a motorized hood replaced two pneumatic positions, and the climber got bearing blocks and flat strap because sliders bound and spools wound unevenly. For Houston: a color sensor, and re-homing after shots because the hood belt slipped.",
        "basis": "team",
        "src": [
          "s37",
          "s31",
          "s30",
          "s35"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "outcome": [
      {
        "text": "46-10-2 in official play; won the Roebling division; pick 1 of the Roebling alliance on Einstein; ranked 20 of the pooled top 20 (OPR 56.10, Roebling).",
        "basis": "record",
        "src": [
          "s1",
          "s2"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "functions": [
    {
      "name": "Drivetrain",
      "cost": [
        {
          "text": "With a fixed camera and no turret, accurate odometry is harder to keep through a match",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s34",
            "s40"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "does": [
        "drive"
      ],
      "type": "six-motor west coast drive",
      "serves": [],
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "NEO motors",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s26"
          ],
          "value": 6,
          "unit": "motors"
        },
        {
          "text": "In a January interview: three NEOs per side, omni wheels on the corners and traction wheels in the middle (auto-captions)",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s23"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Frame and layout",
      "solves": [
        {
          "text": "Let parts be adjusted easily when something does not work or breaks",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s26"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "does": [
        "structure"
      ],
      "type": "gusset and tube construction",
      "serves": [],
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Length x width x height 30 x 29 x 40 in the February CAD post (units not stated)",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s26"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "CAD weight in February without battery or bumpers, about",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s26"
          ],
          "value": 94.702,
          "unit": "lb"
        },
        {
          "text": "One ball sits at the top of the tower and one on the curve at its bottom; to minimize the center of gravity, tower height was lowered by",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s41"
          ],
          "value": 6,
          "unit": "in"
        },
        {
          "text": "The red main power wire pulled out of its connector and the robot missed a Granite State qualification match; the team traced it to a crimp tool sized for metric wire",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s52"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Intake",
      "pieces": [
        "cargo"
      ],
      "solves": [
        {
          "text": "Pick cargo off the floor and survive hits by being flexible rather than rigid",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s39",
            "s27"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cost": [
        {
          "text": "At the championship a polycarbonate link cracked, a roller shaft bent, presumably in an impact, and the intake seemed not to work for about 30 s of one match",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s33"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "does": [
        "acquire"
      ],
      "type": "over-bumper roller intake on a four-bar with a surgical tubing link",
      "serves": [
        "hub-upper"
      ],
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "The fourth bar is surgical tubing: slack when stowed, taut when out, pulling the roller plate into place",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s27",
            "s50"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The motor sits inside the frame at the pivot, which keeps the moving part light",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s27"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Cylinders are retracted when the intake is out, to reduce the risk of bending their rods",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s27",
            "s39"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Gear ratio 60:16; the top roller is slightly slower in the hope that fewer balls jump out",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s26"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "A passive dropdown bar, held down by tubing that goes over center, gave the prototype more favorable compression on the bumper",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s26"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Aluminum support gusset added after a part broke in practice",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s39"
          ],
          "value": 0.09,
          "unit": "in"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "\"Hopper\"",
      "pieces": [
        "cargo"
      ],
      "solves": [
        {
          "text": "Center cargo into the tower, including balls that enter at the edges of the intake",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s26"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cost": [
        {
          "text": "A forum post says the intake sometimes had problems taking two cargo at once",
          "basis": "observer",
          "src": [
            "s48"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "does": [
        "transfer"
      ],
      "type": "omni-wheel centering stage",
      "serves": [
        "hub-upper"
      ],
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "It centers cargo and does not store it",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s26"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Two mostly independent halves with one belt across keep the compressor and pneumatic hub reachable",
          "basis": "team",
          "src": [
            "s41"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "A 1678 member who compared the two CAD models gives the omni wheel spacing as",
          "basis": "observer",
          "src": [
            "s46"
          ],
          "value": 12,
          "unit": "in"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
